WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.120
I hope you guys had a wonderful conference so far.

00:03.120 --> 00:05.960
Have a great day ahead.

00:05.960 --> 00:10.280
Welcome to the AV Capsit Engineering 4 session.

00:10.280 --> 00:13.280
I'm Saurav Chaudhary.

00:13.280 --> 00:15.720
Before we begin, I have a couple of quick reminders,

00:15.720 --> 00:18.520
which you've probably heard before.

00:18.520 --> 00:21.240
As a reminder, the use of flash photography and filming

00:21.240 --> 00:24.560
is prohibited during the session.

00:24.560 --> 00:26.840
I'm supposed to tell you that there is Wi-Fi and there

00:26.840 --> 00:29.200
is an app, but if you have not used it, that's why you're

00:29.200 --> 00:32.800
not going to use it now, so it should be good.

00:32.800 --> 00:34.840
And your feedback is incredibly useful to us.

00:34.840 --> 00:36.600
I'll circle back on that topic at the end.

00:36.600 --> 00:39.400
So without further ado, it's my pleasure

00:39.400 --> 00:42.720
to welcome Tyler Moyr from Voyager Therapeutics.

00:42.720 --> 00:45.000
He's going to talk to us about the directed evolution

00:45.000 --> 00:47.320
of muscular and neuromuscular capsid variants

00:47.320 --> 00:50.360
in both mice and non-human primates.

00:50.360 --> 00:51.160
Tyler, take it away.

01:00.200 --> 01:02.280
All right, thank you for that introduction,

01:02.280 --> 01:04.800
and thank you to the organizers for allowing me

01:04.800 --> 01:07.720
to share this work with you all that we've

01:07.720 --> 01:10.080
been working on for the past few years.

01:10.080 --> 01:11.440
What I'm going to talk about today

01:11.440 --> 01:13.760
is actually probably a bit of a different direction

01:13.760 --> 01:16.080
for Voyager Therapeutics.

01:16.080 --> 01:18.280
If you're familiar with our work, you know,

01:18.280 --> 01:21.960
of our work more in the CNS and gene therapy space

01:21.960 --> 01:23.600
for neurodegenerative disorders.

01:23.600 --> 01:26.040
But today, I'm going to talk about some work

01:26.040 --> 01:29.080
that we've done developing novel capsids for muscular

01:29.120 --> 01:31.280
and neuromuscular indications.

01:34.880 --> 01:37.840
So at Voyager, we've developed the tracer platform,

01:37.840 --> 01:42.840
which is an RNA-based AAV capsid evolution platform

01:43.200 --> 01:48.200
that allows us to evolve and engineer capsids

01:48.600 --> 01:53.600
with tissue-specific targeting for maximal gene delivery

01:53.640 --> 01:56.400
to a given tissue of interest.

01:56.400 --> 01:57.880
And of course, as I mentioned,

01:57.880 --> 02:00.360
we first applied the tracer platform

02:00.360 --> 02:05.360
to engineer transformative BBB penetrant AAV vectors

02:05.520 --> 02:07.760
for CNS-specific disorders.

02:08.760 --> 02:11.000
And on that note, I hope you all got the opportunity

02:11.000 --> 02:13.040
to hear our chief scientific officer,

02:13.040 --> 02:15.960
Todd Carter, present this past Wednesday morning

02:15.960 --> 02:19.040
about the development of VY-1706.

02:19.040 --> 02:20.920
And that is our gene therapy product

02:20.920 --> 02:23.360
for Alzheimer's disease.

02:23.360 --> 02:24.640
It's a tau knockdown.

02:25.520 --> 02:27.440
We're really excited about the three-month GLP

02:27.440 --> 02:29.960
tox data that he presented,

02:29.960 --> 02:33.280
and we're hoping to take that into the clinic later this year.

02:33.280 --> 02:34.520
So please stay tuned.

02:36.640 --> 02:40.400
But of course, outside of the CNS and beyond the BBB,

02:40.400 --> 02:43.160
there is need across many therapeutic areas

02:43.160 --> 02:47.360
for better, safer, stronger AAV capsids

02:47.360 --> 02:49.320
for gene therapy programs.

02:49.320 --> 02:52.400
And that's what I'm going to talk about here momentarily.

02:52.840 --> 02:57.840
So given that this is capsid engineering four,

02:58.920 --> 03:01.360
by now I presume many of you are familiar with the

03:01.360 --> 03:06.200
and overall capsid engineering platform overview.

03:07.560 --> 03:12.280
So for us, instead of processing brain tissue,

03:12.280 --> 03:14.680
we can easily just choose another tissue of interest.

03:14.680 --> 03:18.440
And now we've begun working in skeletal muscle

03:18.440 --> 03:20.360
and cardiac muscle.

03:20.360 --> 03:22.240
So we did this over the past few years.

03:22.240 --> 03:24.160
We launched a number of campaigns

03:24.160 --> 03:27.720
to identify muscle-specific capsids.

03:27.720 --> 03:31.840
And here I'm showing the results of one of our campaigns.

03:31.840 --> 03:36.440
We're looking at the enrichment of a number of pooled variants

03:36.440 --> 03:40.360
in a synomologous macaque muscle and mouse muscle

03:40.360 --> 03:42.000
relative to AAV9.

03:42.000 --> 03:45.120
And we were able to uncover a host of variants

03:45.120 --> 03:47.760
that showed enrichment in the muscles

03:47.760 --> 03:49.400
in both of those species.

03:49.400 --> 03:50.480
And in fact, in the monkeys,

03:50.720 --> 03:54.480
some that were improved upon a known monkey benchmark

03:54.480 --> 03:56.240
muscle capsid.

03:56.240 --> 03:59.440
So we chose a collection of these to begin evaluating

03:59.440 --> 04:01.680
and we were able to quite easily evaluate a lot of them

04:01.680 --> 04:04.060
in mice, so that's where I'll start.

04:05.640 --> 04:10.640
So relative to AAV9, we examined a bunch of capsids

04:11.080 --> 04:15.080
and when we looked at whole body and vivo imaging,

04:15.080 --> 04:18.360
we had a number of capsids that had substantial

04:18.400 --> 04:22.960
muscle targeting globally in muscles relative to AAV9.

04:22.960 --> 04:25.400
And they were on the order or in some cases,

04:25.400 --> 04:29.280
better than the benchmark capsid that we evaluated.

04:30.640 --> 04:32.920
So we chose one of these capsids.

04:32.920 --> 04:36.120
We denote it VCAP290 and we'll take a closer look

04:36.120 --> 04:36.920
at that one.

04:38.080 --> 04:41.160
So when we looked at VCAP290's ability

04:41.160 --> 04:44.400
to deliver a transgene here to the muscles,

04:44.400 --> 04:46.600
what we saw was just really a substantial increase

04:46.600 --> 04:51.360
over AAV9 in all muscle tissues that we examined.

04:51.360 --> 04:53.720
And again, sort of at the level or better

04:53.720 --> 04:55.680
than a benchmark capsid.

04:55.680 --> 05:00.160
And that was confirmed when we did more bioanalytics on it.

05:00.160 --> 05:02.960
In mice, VCAP290 had about 100-fold improvement

05:02.960 --> 05:05.840
in expression over AAV9.

05:05.840 --> 05:09.200
In heart tissue, it was about 10-fold improved.

05:09.200 --> 05:10.680
And we want to note that in liver,

05:10.680 --> 05:14.760
we actually did see a mild detargeting

05:14.760 --> 05:17.660
in terms of biodistribution to the liver.

05:18.680 --> 05:22.080
Now, when we first looked at these muscle capsids,

05:22.080 --> 05:25.460
we were at least these images, these histology images,

05:25.460 --> 05:28.000
we were really struck and it looked at this global view

05:28.000 --> 05:31.360
like we were really hitting every single muscle fiber

05:31.360 --> 05:33.080
and at this dose.

05:33.080 --> 05:36.400
And we confirmed that when we zoomed in a bit

05:36.400 --> 05:41.400
in skeletal muscle at this dose of 2.5 E13 VGs per KIG,

05:41.720 --> 05:44.320
which therapeutically would say is a low dose

05:44.320 --> 05:47.000
compared to what's being used in the clinic,

05:47.000 --> 05:48.960
we were hitting every muscle fiber.

05:49.840 --> 05:52.080
So we decided to take that even lower

05:52.080 --> 05:56.160
and we did a five-fold reduction in dose

05:56.160 --> 05:59.000
down to five E12 VGs per KIG.

05:59.000 --> 06:02.040
And again, we were able to hit 100%

06:02.040 --> 06:04.680
of skeletal muscle fibers,

06:04.680 --> 06:07.440
although we are seeing more variation

06:07.440 --> 06:09.960
in on a fiber-by-fiber basis.

06:10.800 --> 06:15.760
So at this point, I'm gonna switch over to our evaluation

06:15.760 --> 06:19.160
of VCAP290 in cinemulgus macaques

06:19.160 --> 06:21.520
and we switched trans genes here

06:21.520 --> 06:24.880
and we go into what we presented a few years ago

06:24.880 --> 06:28.440
as what we call sort of our multi-tag system.

06:28.440 --> 06:31.660
This is our ability to take a number of capsids of interest.

06:31.660 --> 06:34.400
In this case, we're gonna look at AEV9,

06:34.400 --> 06:39.000
a primate benchmark muscle capsid and VCAP290

06:39.000 --> 06:43.240
and we put them head-to-head in the exact same macaques.

06:43.240 --> 06:47.360
Each capsid is dosed pretty low at four E12 VGs per KIG

06:47.360 --> 06:50.040
and each is packaged with a histone trans gene,

06:50.040 --> 06:52.240
each with a unique epitope tag

06:52.240 --> 06:54.440
so that we can differentiate the capsids

06:54.440 --> 06:57.820
based on in histology or bioanalytics.

06:59.000 --> 07:01.120
And another note before I show the data

07:01.120 --> 07:03.560
is that with a histone trans gene,

07:03.560 --> 07:06.320
we aren't gonna see that fiber-wide staining

07:06.320 --> 07:09.080
like we saw in the mouse with the cytosolic protein.

07:09.080 --> 07:11.240
You're just gonna see targeting in the nuclei

07:11.240 --> 07:14.080
that are squeezed up against the cell membrane there

07:14.080 --> 07:15.360
in the muscle fibers.

07:16.720 --> 07:20.360
But nevertheless, we can still quantify

07:20.360 --> 07:22.840
the targeting to the muscle tissue

07:22.840 --> 07:25.640
and in the macaque quadriceps,

07:25.640 --> 07:29.280
we were able to see about 30% of nuclei being targeted

07:29.280 --> 07:33.200
by VCAP290 as opposed to about 6% in AEV9.

07:33.320 --> 07:34.360
And in terms of expression,

07:34.360 --> 07:37.640
we saw about 25-fold increase

07:37.640 --> 07:41.320
in trans gene expression relative to AEV9,

07:41.320 --> 07:43.800
both of those metrics exceeding the benchmark capsid

07:43.800 --> 07:44.840
that we evaluated.

07:46.920 --> 07:48.560
When we look at heart,

07:49.680 --> 07:53.880
we saw a similar thing where VCAP290

07:53.880 --> 07:57.080
was hitting about 20% of nuclei in the heart

07:57.080 --> 07:59.000
at this dose of 40-12

07:59.000 --> 08:02.600
and that led to about a 13-fold increase

08:02.600 --> 08:05.360
in expression relative to AEV9.

08:07.480 --> 08:10.680
We also evaluated some peripheral tissues.

08:11.760 --> 08:15.320
So in the DRGs, we noted a mild detargeting

08:15.320 --> 08:18.560
with VCAP290 relative to AEV9,

08:18.560 --> 08:20.960
but in the liver, we actually did see an increase

08:20.960 --> 08:25.280
in targeting, but we really saw that as an opportunity

08:25.280 --> 08:28.440
to continue to develop VCAP290

08:28.440 --> 08:32.160
and push it into a second generation of evolution.

08:32.160 --> 08:37.160
So we performed an evolution scheme with VCAP290

08:37.800 --> 08:41.840
and we really focused choosing capsids to move forward with

08:41.840 --> 08:46.840
that had increased expression in muscle tissue in monkeys,

08:47.360 --> 08:51.280
which I'm showing here, but also mouse as well,

08:51.280 --> 08:54.480
but detargeting of those peripheral tissues like the liver.

08:55.280 --> 08:57.880
We're well underway with evaluating a number of these.

08:57.880 --> 09:00.800
We have some exciting data thus far

09:00.800 --> 09:03.720
and we hope to share that in the future.

09:06.320 --> 09:11.200
But I'm gonna switch gears just a little bit

09:11.200 --> 09:13.920
and talk about neuromuscular capsids.

09:13.920 --> 09:18.440
And the inspiration for our work here is that

09:18.440 --> 09:22.120
we took a look and we have done a lot of work

09:22.120 --> 09:25.240
in the CNS space and we've developed some great capsids

09:25.240 --> 09:28.240
that we're excited about like VCAP Gen2.

09:28.240 --> 09:31.800
And I just described our work that we are now developing

09:31.800 --> 09:34.800
muscle capsids like VCAP290.

09:34.800 --> 09:36.840
But of course, in between these two ideas,

09:36.840 --> 09:40.760
there's a therapeutic need for neuromuscular disorders

09:40.760 --> 09:45.560
where you want capsids that have broad CNS transduction

09:45.560 --> 09:49.480
as well as enhanced muscle tropism.

09:49.480 --> 09:52.600
So we set out to evolve or engineer capsids

09:52.600 --> 09:54.440
that are capable of this.

09:54.560 --> 09:59.040
And I'm gonna share results of two separate capsids today.

09:59.040 --> 10:03.520
These are VCAP336 and 337.

10:03.520 --> 10:06.320
So VCAP336 is really just gonna be our first

10:06.320 --> 10:08.240
sort of proof of concept in mouse.

10:08.240 --> 10:13.240
And then 337 is the capsid that we evaluate in primates.

10:14.440 --> 10:17.120
So I'll start with VCAP336.

10:18.760 --> 10:23.480
So we're gonna compare it in the benchmark muscle mouse

10:23.480 --> 10:28.480
capsid as well as VCAP Gen2 our own muscle capsid

10:29.160 --> 10:30.600
or brain capsid.

10:30.600 --> 10:32.080
And we can see that the benchmark capsid

10:32.080 --> 10:34.320
when you look at skeletal muscle or heart tissue,

10:34.320 --> 10:36.000
you get really strong targeting.

10:36.000 --> 10:39.200
Not so much in the motor cortex that are in the brain.

10:39.200 --> 10:42.240
VCAP Gen2 of course has the exact opposite phenotype

10:42.240 --> 10:45.040
where we don't see much targeting in muscles,

10:45.040 --> 10:46.520
but certainly in the motor cortex

10:46.520 --> 10:48.800
we see quite a strong tropism.

10:48.800 --> 10:49.800
And of course the question is,

10:49.800 --> 10:52.440
do we get a best of both worlds capsid

10:52.480 --> 10:55.280
where we combine those phenotypes?

10:55.280 --> 10:58.560
And in mice that's what we saw.

10:58.560 --> 11:02.800
With VCAP336 we were able to successfully increase

11:02.800 --> 11:05.840
the targeting in the skeletal muscle and heart,

11:05.840 --> 11:07.240
certainly relative to the baseline

11:07.240 --> 11:09.520
of where the brain capsid was.

11:09.520 --> 11:12.320
And in the cortex we still have a really strong targeting.

11:12.320 --> 11:14.360
You can see there in the histology,

11:14.360 --> 11:18.800
although we note that in terms of expression in the brain

11:18.800 --> 11:21.360
we did take a little bit of a hit.

11:21.360 --> 11:25.160
But nevertheless I think this validates our approach

11:25.160 --> 11:28.360
that we are in fact getting a best of both worlds capsid

11:28.360 --> 11:31.920
that we can target both of these tissues simultaneously

11:31.920 --> 11:33.140
with one product.

11:35.280 --> 11:37.640
And interestingly in the liver,

11:37.640 --> 11:41.560
we noted that VCAP336 took on the liver phenotype

11:41.560 --> 11:46.560
more so of our CNS capsids, which are quite liver detargeted.

11:48.920 --> 11:50.560
So switching over to monkeys,

11:50.600 --> 11:54.040
we're gonna revisit that multi-tag system

11:54.040 --> 11:56.840
that I described where we do a head-to-head evaluation

11:56.840 --> 11:59.720
in the same primates of various capsids.

11:59.720 --> 12:04.200
So here we're using AAV9, a benchmark muscle capsid,

12:04.200 --> 12:08.000
and our neuromuscular capsid VCAP337.

12:09.320 --> 12:10.560
So in terms of the quadriceps,

12:10.560 --> 12:13.960
we saw an increase with VCAP337 relative to the benchmark

12:13.960 --> 12:17.320
in terms of its ability to target the cells.

12:17.320 --> 12:20.440
We're seeing an increase in the amount of nuclei targeted.

12:20.440 --> 12:23.440
Although we do note that we saw a little bit of a reduction

12:23.440 --> 12:26.600
there in the expression in the quadriceps.

12:27.600 --> 12:31.200
But in the heart, we were able to see an increase

12:31.200 --> 12:33.120
in both of those metrics.

12:33.120 --> 12:38.120
337 targeted a higher amount of nuclei in the heart

12:38.560 --> 12:40.120
as well as increased this expression

12:40.120 --> 12:43.720
about 10-fold relative to that of AAV9.

12:44.800 --> 12:47.000
But I think the brain is actually where we see

12:47.000 --> 12:48.640
our most exciting data.

12:49.920 --> 12:54.920
So VCAP337 in the motor cortex of these primates

12:55.640 --> 12:59.840
had upwards of 50% of nuclei being targeted

12:59.840 --> 13:01.120
in the motor cortex.

13:01.120 --> 13:03.120
And that led to about, that was measured

13:03.120 --> 13:08.120
at about a 250-fold increase in AAV9 over,

13:08.280 --> 13:10.880
or with VCAP337.

13:12.720 --> 13:14.600
Now if we compare that to VCAPGEN2,

13:14.600 --> 13:15.840
and I'll make the stipulation here

13:15.880 --> 13:18.640
that VCAPGEN2 is data taken

13:18.640 --> 13:20.520
from some historical data that we have,

13:20.520 --> 13:23.920
and we presented years ago here at ASGCT.

13:23.920 --> 13:26.720
But in terms of the number of nuclei targeted

13:26.720 --> 13:29.880
and the amount of expression that we're seeing,

13:29.880 --> 13:32.520
we're more than doubling in either case,

13:32.520 --> 13:34.560
which we think is really exciting.

13:37.160 --> 13:40.200
In the liver, we see the same thing that we saw on mouse

13:40.200 --> 13:44.120
in that VCAP337 is largely reduced

13:44.120 --> 13:47.840
and more similar to our CNS crossing capsids

13:47.840 --> 13:50.440
than they are the muscle capsids.

13:50.440 --> 13:55.440
I will make the disclaimer disclosure here

13:55.440 --> 14:00.440
that the brain targeting mechanism of both 336 and 337

14:00.560 --> 14:03.160
is in fact ALPL, that is the mechanism

14:03.160 --> 14:06.300
by which they are getting into the BBB.

14:08.560 --> 14:10.240
And we are continuing to evolve these

14:10.240 --> 14:13.480
where we have next generation variants

14:14.240 --> 14:17.360
beyond 336 and 337 that we're currently evaluating.

14:18.680 --> 14:22.080
So to just give you a summary of what I described here,

14:22.080 --> 14:25.480
we've developed muscle capsids with VCAP290,

14:25.480 --> 14:27.360
has about a hundred fold increase in expression

14:27.360 --> 14:30.200
over AAV9 in mouse skeletal muscle,

14:30.200 --> 14:32.640
10 fold in cardiac muscle.

14:32.640 --> 14:35.240
In primates, we're looking at 25 fold over AAV9

14:35.240 --> 14:39.360
in the skeletal muscle and about 13 fold in heart.

14:39.360 --> 14:41.160
At low doses, we can see that we're targeting

14:41.160 --> 14:43.840
100% of muscle fibers in the mice.

14:43.840 --> 14:46.920
And we've moved on to developing neuromuscular capsids

14:46.920 --> 14:50.240
that we think have sort of the best of both worlds there

14:50.240 --> 14:54.200
with VCAP336 and 337 and those are liver-detargeted

14:55.680 --> 14:58.240
more similar to our brain capsids.

14:59.080 --> 15:02.080
So with that, I wanna thank all my colleagues

15:02.080 --> 15:04.280
at Voyager, current and former.

15:05.320 --> 15:07.640
As you all know, these projects take a lot of brain,

15:07.640 --> 15:12.320
a lot of muscle, so it's gonna give a shout out to them.

15:12.320 --> 15:14.400
And with whatever time is remaining,

15:14.400 --> 15:16.400
I'll be happy to take a question or two.

15:18.280 --> 15:23.280
I think we have time for one good question.

15:26.360 --> 15:28.520
I tell you, thanks, really exciting.

15:28.520 --> 15:30.800
I was wondering, does 336 work in NHP

15:30.800 --> 15:32.840
and does 337 work in mice?

15:34.200 --> 15:38.300
We did not test them in the other species.

15:39.800 --> 15:42.280
So I just can't say for certain.

15:42.280 --> 15:44.560
I think to some degree they both would

15:44.560 --> 15:47.040
work in the other species, but to what degree

15:47.040 --> 15:50.560
I just can't say because we didn't do that experiment.

15:50.560 --> 15:53.240
Moving forward, the next generation of variants

15:53.240 --> 15:58.240
that we are evaluating, we're same capsid, both animals.

15:58.680 --> 15:59.520
Thanks.

16:01.520 --> 16:02.360
Thank you, Tyler.

16:04.600 --> 16:09.600
Our next speaker is Nolan Graham from UC San Diego.

16:10.600 --> 16:13.280
He'll be talking about receptor-guided

16:13.280 --> 16:16.160
avetropism engineering via MATCH.

16:16.160 --> 16:17.400
Thank you.

16:17.400 --> 16:18.240
Awesome.

16:23.560 --> 16:25.200
Hello everyone, my name's Nolan Graham.

16:25.200 --> 16:27.680
I'm a PhD student in the lab of Dr. Prashant Mali

16:27.680 --> 16:30.960
at UC San Diego and today I'll be talking about my research,

16:30.960 --> 16:33.640
which is titled receptor-guided avetropism engineering

16:33.640 --> 16:34.480
via MATCH.

16:36.080 --> 16:38.680
So several of the main issues with AV gene therapy,

16:38.680 --> 16:40.240
including high cost and toxicity,

16:40.240 --> 16:41.720
stand to be overcome through the development

16:41.720 --> 16:44.160
of more specific, efficient vectors.

16:45.320 --> 16:46.880
Classically, these are identified

16:46.880 --> 16:49.920
through large-scale screens, which are resource-intensive

16:49.920 --> 16:53.440
and generally limited by the intolerance of the AAV capsid

16:53.440 --> 16:55.600
to larger peptide insertions.

16:55.600 --> 16:58.760
So previously, we've incorporated unnatural amino acids

16:58.760 --> 17:01.280
and peptide tags into the AAV capsid.

17:01.280 --> 17:03.240
And based on this, we developed the MATCH system,

17:03.240 --> 17:05.120
which stands for modulation of AAV tropism

17:05.120 --> 17:07.600
through conjugation to homing proteins.

17:07.640 --> 17:09.720
Specifically, we wanted to redirect the tropism

17:09.720 --> 17:13.040
of a couple of different AAVs to desired targets

17:13.040 --> 17:16.280
by conjugating, targeting ligands to the capsid

17:16.280 --> 17:18.680
via spy tags by catcher chemistry.

17:20.680 --> 17:24.200
So to start, we went into AAV DJ and inserted a spy tag

17:24.200 --> 17:27.280
into one of the protruding loop regions on the VP.

17:27.280 --> 17:29.360
This was flanked by flexible GS linkers,

17:29.360 --> 17:30.440
and then we incorporated this

17:30.440 --> 17:33.200
into a fully spy tag containing capsid.

17:33.200 --> 17:35.280
These capsids were reacted overnight

17:35.360 --> 17:37.680
at room temperature with spy catcher.

17:38.720 --> 17:42.720
And upon doing so, we saw a shift in the size of the VP

17:43.680 --> 17:46.440
as a result of that covalent spy catcher attachment.

17:47.960 --> 17:49.440
We took these to TEM to ensure

17:49.440 --> 17:52.600
that we were still forming fully formed capsids.

17:52.600 --> 17:54.560
And then upon spy catcher binding,

17:54.560 --> 17:56.360
they maintained their integrity

17:56.360 --> 17:58.440
with a slight topology change.

17:58.440 --> 18:01.640
And upon going to DLS, we saw that there was a slight shift

18:01.640 --> 18:03.520
in the mean diameter of these capsids.

18:04.520 --> 18:06.400
So we wanted to preempt an issue at this point

18:06.400 --> 18:09.480
that we foresaw where for covering the entire AAV capsid

18:09.480 --> 18:13.760
with spy catcher, we wouldn't be allowing the binding

18:13.760 --> 18:16.440
of secondary receptors such as AAVR,

18:16.440 --> 18:17.760
which are required for the AAV

18:17.760 --> 18:20.840
to effectively transduce and produce.

18:21.760 --> 18:24.880
So to do this, we put together a panel of mosaic AAVs

18:24.880 --> 18:28.120
with various ratios of AAV DJ to AAV spy tag

18:28.120 --> 18:31.160
with many of them primarily composed of AAV DJ.

18:32.160 --> 18:36.560
In hella cells, when we transduced in vitro,

18:36.560 --> 18:39.080
we saw that the fully spy tag containing capsids

18:39.080 --> 18:41.320
lost nearly all of the transduction ability

18:41.320 --> 18:44.000
that native AAV DJ has.

18:44.000 --> 18:47.920
And this is likely due to the insertion of the spy tag peptide

18:47.920 --> 18:51.600
immediately in the heparin sulfate proteoglycan binding domain

18:51.600 --> 18:52.560
of AAV DJ.

18:53.520 --> 18:55.120
Interestingly, in the mosaic capsids,

18:55.120 --> 18:57.400
we also saw a drop in transduction ability

18:57.400 --> 18:58.480
upon spy catcher binding,

18:58.480 --> 19:00.320
which is likely due to that steric occlusion

19:00.320 --> 19:03.080
of binding sites that I mentioned before.

19:04.000 --> 19:06.920
So we took our five to one and 11 to one mosaic capsids

19:06.920 --> 19:08.520
and decided to start targeting.

19:09.600 --> 19:11.440
So first we went into resting PBMCs,

19:11.440 --> 19:12.920
which are classically a challenging target

19:12.920 --> 19:14.520
for AAV transduction,

19:14.520 --> 19:18.080
and attached either a CD3 or CD28 SCFEs

19:18.080 --> 19:20.000
to the outside of the capsid.

19:20.000 --> 19:23.040
And saw that the CD3 targeting SCFEs,

19:23.040 --> 19:24.560
particularly the SCFE1,

19:24.560 --> 19:26.680
were able to transduce the sizable portion

19:26.680 --> 19:31.080
of the resting PBMCs without prior activation.

19:31.080 --> 19:32.880
We also wanted to go for the brain

19:32.880 --> 19:35.880
and to do so attached a murine transferrin receptor binding

19:35.880 --> 19:37.960
SCFE to the outside of the capsid

19:37.960 --> 19:40.080
and injected these retroorbitally in mice.

19:41.120 --> 19:43.400
And saw that decorated capsids were able to transduce

19:43.400 --> 19:47.200
the brain much more effectively than AAV DJ by itself.

19:48.560 --> 19:51.480
So we wanted to bring this into a more popular

19:52.840 --> 19:55.120
wild type AAV to be more therapeutically relevant.

19:55.160 --> 19:58.320
And to do so, we decided to make match AAV9.

19:58.320 --> 20:01.200
AAV9 is a wild type AAV that's already used

20:01.200 --> 20:04.320
to target the CNS with engineered capsids.

20:04.320 --> 20:07.080
And it uses a different primary receptor from AAV DJ

20:07.080 --> 20:08.920
in that it uses galactose to bind

20:08.920 --> 20:11.760
as opposed to upper and sulfate proteoglycan.

20:11.760 --> 20:14.680
So to do so, we inserted our spot tag

20:14.680 --> 20:17.280
into both loop one and loop two,

20:17.280 --> 20:20.360
which are protruding loop regions on the VP,

20:20.360 --> 20:23.720
and flank these by either 1X or 2X flexible linkers.

20:23.720 --> 20:27.320
And upon forming full capsids with these spot tag

20:27.320 --> 20:30.760
containing VP's, we saw that both L1 and L2,

20:30.760 --> 20:35.760
loop 2X contained, were able to readily bind spot catching.

20:38.440 --> 20:40.120
Additionally, when we incorporated these

20:40.120 --> 20:42.520
into mosaic AAVs at a ratio of 11 to one,

20:42.520 --> 20:45.600
they produced capsids with titers similar

20:45.600 --> 20:47.000
to that of wild type AAV9.

20:48.640 --> 20:50.360
So we went back to our T cell targeting

20:50.360 --> 20:55.360
and used our CD3 SEFE1 and saw that upon transduction,

20:56.040 --> 20:58.720
we were able to transduce over 50%

20:58.720 --> 21:00.640
of the resting PBMC population.

21:02.600 --> 21:05.120
Additionally, when we went to murine transferrin,

21:06.240 --> 21:08.360
we were able to gain transduction ability

21:08.360 --> 21:10.320
of the brain almost a hundredfold higher

21:10.320 --> 21:12.520
than native AAV9.

21:13.680 --> 21:15.200
Based on this success, we wanted to bring this

21:15.200 --> 21:18.000
into a more therapeutically relevant space

21:18.000 --> 21:20.920
using human transferrin receptor targeting.

21:20.920 --> 21:23.040
And to do so, we put together a panel

21:23.040 --> 21:25.640
from the literature of SEFE's that are targeting

21:25.640 --> 21:30.360
human transferrin receptor, and used HEC cells

21:30.360 --> 21:34.960
that had either been received a safe harbor AAVS1 locust

21:34.960 --> 21:36.880
knockout or a transferrin receptor knockout

21:36.880 --> 21:41.680
via Linty CRISPR, and saw in our AAVS1 knockout cells

21:41.680 --> 21:43.560
that we were able to transduce these cells

21:43.560 --> 21:45.360
much more efficiently than AAV9,

21:45.360 --> 21:49.480
and also with efficacy comparable to that of BIHDFR1,

21:49.480 --> 21:51.480
which is this recently engineered

21:51.480 --> 21:53.800
human transferrin targeting AAV.

21:53.800 --> 21:56.000
This improvement was attenuated in the cells

21:56.000 --> 21:58.160
that received a transferrin receptor knockout,

21:58.160 --> 22:00.160
supporting the concept that we're using

22:00.160 --> 22:02.520
the transferrin receptor to do transduce.

22:04.560 --> 22:08.720
So we took this in vivo with SEFE9 and SEFE12,

22:08.720 --> 22:11.280
and used mice that express the apical domain

22:11.280 --> 22:13.520
of the human transferrin receptor,

22:13.520 --> 22:16.080
as opposed to the murine, and saw

22:16.080 --> 22:22.000
that particularly SEFE12 yielded DNA-level and mRNA-level

22:22.000 --> 22:25.640
transduction comparable to that of BIHDFR1.

22:25.640 --> 22:28.240
We looked at the protein level next, using IHC,

22:28.240 --> 22:31.760
and saw that we were getting brain-wide distribution

22:31.760 --> 22:35.920
of our transgene, again comparable to that of BIHDFR1.

22:35.920 --> 22:38.600
So this was pretty promising, but before we proceeded,

22:38.600 --> 22:41.160
we wanted to ensure that this was more applicable

22:41.160 --> 22:43.920
for many other labs.

22:43.920 --> 22:47.560
So to do so, up until this point,

22:47.560 --> 22:50.640
everything had been produced, either separately

22:50.640 --> 22:53.760
with separate protein production from AAV production

22:53.760 --> 22:58.280
as a kind of modular system, where you could retarget AAVs.

22:58.280 --> 22:59.640
The same AAV to different targets,

22:59.640 --> 23:00.960
depending on what you conjugate.

23:00.960 --> 23:02.360
But at this point, we wanted to ensure

23:02.360 --> 23:05.320
that we could make everything together

23:05.320 --> 23:07.760
in a triple-transfection style approach.

23:07.760 --> 23:11.360
So we have our standard triple-transfection plasmids,

23:11.360 --> 23:14.720
which I'm sure many of you are familiar with.

23:14.720 --> 23:16.840
And this also includes our spy tag rep cap,

23:16.840 --> 23:19.240
which we've used up until this point in the mosaics,

23:19.240 --> 23:21.840
and then also our spy catcher ligand.

23:21.840 --> 23:24.000
So when we did this with the CD3 targeting SEFE,

23:24.000 --> 23:28.040
we got titers, similar to the wild type AAV9, once again.

23:28.040 --> 23:32.480
And when we took this in vitro, with our L1 and L2,

23:32.480 --> 23:34.160
5 to 1 and 11 to 1 mosaics, we were

23:34.160 --> 23:38.680
able to transduce a large portion of the T-cell population,

23:38.680 --> 23:41.680
or PVMC population.

23:41.680 --> 23:43.480
So in conclusion, the match AAV platform

23:43.480 --> 23:46.440
overcomes a major challenge in the AAV engineering

23:46.440 --> 23:48.960
by allowing us to put full-size proteins on the outside

23:48.960 --> 23:51.440
of the AAV.

23:51.440 --> 23:53.760
Through a series of small-scale binder screens,

23:53.760 --> 23:56.040
we identified T-cell and brain-cell targeting motifs

23:56.040 --> 23:58.840
that were pulled straight from the literature.

23:58.840 --> 24:01.000
And one of these in CD3 targeted vectors,

24:01.040 --> 24:04.600
we're able to transduce a majority of the PVMC population,

24:04.600 --> 24:07.680
while MTFR1 and HDFR1 targeted vectors

24:07.680 --> 24:10.560
transduce the brain effectively with much higher

24:10.560 --> 24:13.560
transduction ability than AAV9 and comparable transduction

24:13.560 --> 24:17.360
to that of popular engineered AAVs.

24:17.360 --> 24:20.160
And finally, the mix and match vector productions

24:20.160 --> 24:23.760
enabled one-pot generation of these targeted vectors

24:23.760 --> 24:26.000
in a method that many labs will be familiar with using

24:26.000 --> 24:29.760
the standard triple-transfection protocols.

24:29.760 --> 24:33.720
So with that, I'd like to thank my mentor, Dr. Mali,

24:33.720 --> 24:36.600
as well as my other co-authors in bold,

24:36.600 --> 24:38.560
and as well as the rest of the lab.

24:38.560 --> 24:41.320
This work has recently been accepted for publication,

24:41.320 --> 24:43.160
so be sure to follow up.

24:43.160 --> 24:46.160
Thank you.

24:46.160 --> 24:52.160
Thank you.

24:52.160 --> 24:53.160
Thanks, Nolan.

24:53.160 --> 24:54.640
The floor is open for questions.

24:54.640 --> 24:55.640
Is there any?

25:00.320 --> 25:02.880
Very quick questions.

25:02.880 --> 25:08.640
Do you have the characterization of your...

25:08.640 --> 25:10.520
It's called match AAV.

25:10.520 --> 25:14.560
Yes, how many copies of the antibody

25:14.600 --> 25:17.240
decorated of your AAV?

25:17.240 --> 25:21.440
So we're not able to directly quantify that yet.

25:21.440 --> 25:24.160
Like how many SCFEs per AAV.

25:24.160 --> 25:25.840
However, based on the kind of stochastic nature

25:25.840 --> 25:28.720
of AAV assembly, we hope that it's relatively

25:28.720 --> 25:32.160
similar to what we transfect at.

25:32.160 --> 25:35.960
How about the full partial analytics?

25:35.960 --> 25:38.960
Like, do you see a lot of full vector genome

25:38.960 --> 25:42.040
being packaged with your novel AAV?

25:42.040 --> 25:44.400
We haven't looked at that yet, but that's a great question.

25:44.560 --> 25:47.800
Thank you.

25:47.800 --> 25:49.120
That's really cool work.

25:49.120 --> 25:53.000
So I was curious, was there a difference in the amount

25:53.000 --> 25:55.920
of spy catcher that conjugated when you did it in vitro

25:55.920 --> 26:00.080
as opposed to when you did it in the cell?

26:00.080 --> 26:04.360
Transduction in vitro versus transduction in the cell?

26:04.360 --> 26:06.080
No, the amount of spy catcher that

26:06.080 --> 26:10.080
bound to the spy tag virus when you did it in vitro

26:10.080 --> 26:13.520
as opposed to when you assembled it within the cell.

26:13.520 --> 26:15.200
Oh, yes.

26:15.200 --> 26:17.680
So we use a large excess, massive excess,

26:17.680 --> 26:21.640
for all the conjugation reactions in vitro.

26:21.640 --> 26:25.000
But when we transfect, it's about a quarter.

26:25.000 --> 26:26.920
There's 100-fold excess of protein

26:26.920 --> 26:29.120
when we do it outside the cell.

26:29.120 --> 26:33.120
But in the cell, we just transfect at a certain ratio,

26:33.120 --> 26:35.400
which is about a quarter of the total DNA transfected.

26:35.400 --> 26:39.440
OK, and the second question was, so you use spy catcher 3,

26:39.440 --> 26:40.960
but you use spy tag 1.

26:41.000 --> 26:43.800
Is there a reason you didn't use spy tag 3?

26:43.800 --> 26:47.160
Yes, so spy tag 3 is a little bit longer than spy tag 1.

26:47.160 --> 26:49.720
So spy tag 1 is only 13 amino acids.

26:49.720 --> 26:53.040
And based on the cross reactivity of spy catcher 3

26:53.040 --> 26:56.600
and spy tag 1, we elected to ensure that our AVs would

26:56.600 --> 27:00.800
package using a smaller peptide insertion.

27:00.800 --> 27:02.880
OK, and the last question is, so when

27:02.880 --> 27:06.560
you did your different ratios, your 11 is to 1,

27:06.560 --> 27:09.800
is that just based on bulk ratios of how you estimated

27:09.800 --> 27:12.240
how many spy tags on each capsid?

27:12.240 --> 27:13.880
Yeah, so we transfect these plasmids

27:13.880 --> 27:17.760
at a molar ratio of 11 to 1 or 5 to 1.

27:17.760 --> 27:22.000
So yeah, the fact that they package similarly

27:22.000 --> 27:24.440
indicates that hopefully they will be at that appropriate

27:24.440 --> 27:26.440
ratio upon assembly.

27:26.440 --> 27:27.440
Thank you.

27:30.120 --> 27:32.480
Great, if there are no further questions, thank you, Nolan.

27:32.480 --> 27:33.480
Thank you.

27:33.960 --> 27:34.960
Thank you.

27:39.960 --> 27:41.840
Our next speaker is Kyle Jue.

27:44.760 --> 27:48.720
I'm waiting for his slides to come up.

27:48.720 --> 27:50.200
There you go.

27:50.200 --> 27:55.040
He'll be talking about ACE502, an AAV5 derived capsid

27:55.040 --> 27:57.160
engineered by InVivo Directed Evolution

27:57.160 --> 28:00.600
for robust CNS transduction and liver detargeting.

28:00.600 --> 28:01.680
Take it away.

28:01.720 --> 28:04.120
OK, thank you so much for your introduction.

28:04.120 --> 28:05.600
So before I start, I want to give thanks

28:05.600 --> 28:08.120
to the organized committee for having

28:08.120 --> 28:13.480
me this great opportunity to share our exciting data on ACE502,

28:13.480 --> 28:18.680
which is the AAV5 derived capsid engineered for CNS,

28:18.680 --> 28:21.560
the robust CNS transduction, and at the same time

28:21.560 --> 28:23.040
the liver detargeting.

28:23.040 --> 28:24.480
So my name is Kyle Jue.

28:24.480 --> 28:29.000
I'm the AAV Capsid Engineering Lead at Amiluid Solution

28:29.040 --> 28:32.200
and also serve as a faculty member at Ewha Women's

28:32.200 --> 28:33.200
University in Kerbya.

28:36.400 --> 28:39.240
Let me briefly introduce our company, the Amiluid Solution.

28:39.240 --> 28:41.640
So you may guess what we are doing from the name.

28:41.640 --> 28:45.440
So Amiluid Solution is just the science-driven biotech,

28:45.440 --> 28:49.880
mainly focused on developing many different disease

28:49.880 --> 28:52.200
modifying kind of the therapies, especially

28:52.200 --> 28:54.400
for Alzheimer's disease.

28:54.400 --> 28:57.480
So basically, we have three different core interconnected

28:57.480 --> 29:00.520
programs, mainly we are focusing on the brain

29:00.520 --> 29:05.880
tropic, AB-based gene therapy kind of approach and platform.

29:05.880 --> 29:09.120
And most of you are working for many different, just

29:09.120 --> 29:13.000
a restoration of clear homeostasis for Alzheimer's

29:13.000 --> 29:14.840
disease treatment.

29:14.840 --> 29:17.720
Also, we are developing some of the diagnostics

29:17.720 --> 29:20.520
for the Alzheimer's as well, too.

29:20.520 --> 29:25.120
So ACE502 is served as a foundational gene delivery

29:25.120 --> 29:27.560
vehicle for our therapeutic programs.

29:30.560 --> 29:33.360
We are mainly working on the Capsid Engineering.

29:33.360 --> 29:38.720
So this is how we discover that these noble caps of the ACE502.

29:38.720 --> 29:41.600
Basically, we are starting from the AB-5 and engineer

29:41.600 --> 29:44.520
two different variable regions at the Capsid

29:44.520 --> 29:49.080
at the same time, variable region four and region eight,

29:49.080 --> 29:51.680
and by substituting the amino acid sequences

29:51.680 --> 29:54.920
by using the random peptide display.

29:54.920 --> 29:57.600
After that, we're using the sequential crucifixes

29:57.600 --> 30:00.400
in vivo screening in order to reduce

30:00.400 --> 30:05.240
the risk of the single model, the selection bias,

30:05.240 --> 30:07.680
and then to make sure that it's a functional

30:07.680 --> 30:09.520
transduction in the brain.

30:09.520 --> 30:14.520
So we just, once more, just pursue the mRNA-based screening

30:14.520 --> 30:18.480
and the evaluation of the late Capsid variants

30:18.480 --> 30:21.960
in non-human primates by using the DNA bar coding.

30:21.960 --> 30:23.640
And after that, the most important question

30:23.640 --> 30:25.560
is a protein-level expression.

30:25.560 --> 30:29.200
So we are just selected the best single, the Capsid,

30:29.200 --> 30:34.120
ACE502, by the individual protein level

30:34.120 --> 30:37.040
head-to-head evaluation and validation

30:37.040 --> 30:42.160
compared to many of the benchmark of capsids.

30:42.160 --> 30:44.600
So based on the sequential, the mouse

30:44.600 --> 30:47.080
to non-human primate in vivo screening,

30:47.080 --> 30:50.480
so we were able to progressively enrich

30:50.520 --> 30:53.680
many different CNS tropic capsid variants

30:53.680 --> 30:57.240
over the AB5 wild time.

30:57.240 --> 31:01.000
So as you can see that AB, ACE502 and 501,

31:01.000 --> 31:02.880
located in a very high fold,

31:02.880 --> 31:05.360
kind of increasing the enrichment

31:05.360 --> 31:07.200
in the non-human primates.

31:07.200 --> 31:10.360
And also, amino acid preference, the HIMMAP,

31:10.360 --> 31:13.160
showing that they conserve the pattern

31:13.160 --> 31:16.520
of the sequences and two different,

31:16.520 --> 31:20.240
the burial region of the capsid of AB5.

31:20.280 --> 31:23.120
So across the species, we have just enrichment

31:23.120 --> 31:25.240
for that many different variants.

31:26.600 --> 31:28.000
And if you're looking a little more close

31:28.000 --> 31:32.200
into our in vivo kind of screening strategy,

31:32.200 --> 31:34.040
so we're starting from the much bigger

31:34.040 --> 31:37.480
and larger kind of volume of the library,

31:37.480 --> 31:39.520
which is 10 to the seven variants.

31:39.520 --> 31:42.840
And after the two round of the mouse in vivo screening,

31:42.840 --> 31:45.800
and then we just selected the 10,000 variants,

31:45.800 --> 31:50.800
which is successfully the transduced mouse brain

31:51.120 --> 31:52.720
and synthesized and then we inject

31:52.720 --> 31:57.360
into the non-human primate is the cynomogous macaque.

31:57.360 --> 32:00.520
And then we further screened them out

32:00.520 --> 32:03.160
by using your selection criteria,

32:03.160 --> 32:06.880
which is the CNS tissue enrichment score,

32:06.880 --> 32:09.600
and also at the same time it's a liberty targeting score,

32:09.600 --> 32:13.240
which is the upper right kind of the corner of the box,

32:13.240 --> 32:17.360
the where we identified our desired variants

32:17.360 --> 32:18.800
that we are looking for.

32:20.960 --> 32:24.440
And based on that, this kind of the in vivo screening,

32:24.440 --> 32:29.280
so we were able to identify top performing 28 capsis.

32:29.280 --> 32:33.280
And then we once again, we just do a mRNA based evaluation

32:33.280 --> 32:35.720
for the lid capsis for make sure

32:35.720 --> 32:38.120
that is a functional transduction.

32:38.120 --> 32:42.480
So as you can see the ACE502 and 501 could achieve

32:43.160 --> 32:46.280
around the 70 fold increase in enrichment

32:46.280 --> 32:50.560
compared to the wild type of AV5 and AV9 as well

32:50.560 --> 32:52.480
in the non-human primates.

32:52.480 --> 32:55.280
And also we co-injected published,

32:55.280 --> 32:58.960
kind of two of the published well-known benchmark capsis

32:58.960 --> 33:01.480
and compared to our capsid.

33:01.480 --> 33:04.960
So interestingly, we can have even higher transduction

33:04.960 --> 33:09.800
compared to those benchmark, the competitor capsis.

33:09.800 --> 33:14.800
And this heat map showing that our ACE502 and 501

33:16.480 --> 33:19.560
could transduce successfully in a different region

33:19.560 --> 33:24.040
of the brain, including the frontal cortex and motor cortex

33:24.040 --> 33:27.640
and deeper brain region, which is hippocampus

33:27.640 --> 33:30.800
and sub-sensional nigra and so on.

33:30.800 --> 33:34.280
And then we see that in peripheral tissues,

33:34.280 --> 33:38.440
including the liver and heart, including the TRG as well.

33:38.440 --> 33:43.440
So ACE501 and 502 is a significantly reduced signal

33:44.040 --> 33:48.520
compared to wild type of the AV5 and AV9

33:48.520 --> 33:50.640
in mRNA level of expression.

33:52.360 --> 33:55.240
So this is comprehensive mRNA expression profile

33:56.520 --> 34:00.760
compares ACE502 against all benchmark capsid

34:00.760 --> 34:02.800
included wild types.

34:02.800 --> 34:07.800
So dark blue is ACE501 and light blue is ACE502.

34:08.480 --> 34:10.920
So you can see that it's consistently maintained

34:10.920 --> 34:15.920
a higher transduction in all the region of the brain

34:16.520 --> 34:20.200
compared to the AV9 wild type from the celibate lung

34:20.200 --> 34:23.760
and cortex and deep brain like a hippocampus

34:23.760 --> 34:27.480
and sub-sensional nigra and thalamus and brain stem

34:27.480 --> 34:30.360
and including the spinal cord as well.

34:30.360 --> 34:33.400
And this baseline is AV5 and then you can see

34:33.400 --> 34:36.080
that green dot is AV9 wild type.

34:36.400 --> 34:38.080
And then you can see the peripheral tissue,

34:38.080 --> 34:42.160
especially in liver, ACE501 and 502,

34:42.160 --> 34:45.600
significantly reduced that mRNA expression

34:45.600 --> 34:48.080
compared to the wild type AV9.

34:48.080 --> 34:50.640
So we believe that it has a feature of the safety

34:50.640 --> 34:52.800
kind of profile of the liver de-targeting.

34:53.940 --> 34:56.040
So we move on to the most important question,

